  "text": {
    "value": "Direct support for visiting GraphQL directives via the `GraphQLTypeVisitor` in graphql-java is a somewhat advanced topic, affected by ongoing API changes over time (notably in v18+). Here's what you need to know based on available information in the repository and discussions:\n\n1. **GraphQLTypeVisitor \u0026 Directives**:  \n   - The visitor pattern in graphql-java (`GraphQLTypeVisitor`) is primarily aimed at traversing schema types (`GraphQLObjectType`, `GraphQLFieldDefinition`, etc.). Its core methods by default do not include a dedicated `visitGraphQLDirective` method for directives themselves.\n   - Instead, directives are associated with types (fields, objects, etc.) and can be accessed through those elements during traversal, but are not visited as standalone nodes via the typical visitor API.\n\n2. **Schema Changes and Migration**:  \n   - Recent graphql-java versions have improved modelling of applied directives. Now, `GraphQLAppliedDirective` and `GraphQLAppliedDirectiveArgument` represent directives as applied to schema elements, separate from directive definitions【3:3†source】.\n   - Due to backwards compatibility, both the old and new approach might result in visitors being called twice for one schema element if you're using both APIs.\n\n3. **Advanced Traversal Notes**:\n   - Currently, if you want to operate on directives via a type visitor, you need to check for directives when visiting the parent container (like `GraphQLFieldDefinition` or `GraphQLObjectType`) using methods like `.getAppliedDirective(\"name\")` or similar (see code samples in discussions)【3:4†source】.\n   - There isn't a `visitGraphQLDirective` out of the box in the `GraphQLTypeVisitor` interface, so you adapt your logic to handle directives when traversing each visited type or field.\n\n4. **References \u0026 Code Search**:\n   - You can directly check the implementation of `GraphQLTypeVisitor` and related traversal code in the repo here:  \n     [graphql-java/src/main/java/graphql/schema/GraphQLTypeVisitor.java](https://github.com/graphql-java/graphql-java/blob/master/src/main/java/graphql/schema/GraphQLTypeVisitor.java)  \n     [graphql-java/src/main/java/graphql/schema/GraphQLAppliedDirective.java](https://github.com/graphql-java/graphql-java/blob/master/src/main/java/graphql/schema/GraphQLAppliedDirective.java)【3:2†source】\n   - There is also a [SchemaTransformer](https://github.com/graphql-java/graphql-java/blob/master/src/main/java/graphql/schema/SchemaTransformer.java) which uses the visitor pattern to allow schema transformation through type visiting【3:6†source】.\n\n**Summary:**  \n- There is no direct `visitGraphQLDirective` method in `GraphQLTypeVisitor`.\n- If you want to \"visit\" directives, check for and process them when visiting parent elements (fields, types, etc.).\n- With newer versions use `GraphQLAppliedDirective` for more accurate, future-proof code.\n\nFor real-world code samples, the repo issues and tests (such as `SchemaGeneratorDirectiveHelperTest.groovy`) often have advanced usage patterns for directive handling in visitors【3:4†source】.",
